$DIR/main.p:14:10: f holds a view of v (the function literal, line 12), which was moved into eat (its parameter takes a L by value) at line 13, so f can no longer be used here
    f looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view was made by the
    function literal at line 12. A view of an owner held in a local variable may
    be used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At
    line 13 v was moved into eat (its parameter takes a L by value), so the view
    may look at memory that was released, or at a value that now belongs to
    something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use f before line 13, or move v only after the last use of f
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
